SOLUTION
The key issue in the previous attempt was a conflict in the syntax used for the custom workspace. While some sources suggest the ${Branch} format doesn't work in Freestyle jobs, a lab-specific solution for this exact KodeKloud scenario confirms that /var/lib/jenkins/${Branch} is the correct and working configuration. The earlier suggestion to use $Branch for the Git branch specifier is also correct.

Here is the definitive, corrected guide to redo the task successfully.

🔌 Step 1: Install Required Plugins
Log in to Jenkins (username admin, password Adm!n321).

Go to Manage Jenkins > Plugins > Available plugins.

Install the Git and Publish Over SSH plugins. Restart Jenkins if prompted.

🔑 Step 2: Configure the SSH Server for Deployment
This is where the previous attempt failed with an "Auth fail" error. The fix is to force password authentication.

Go to Manage Jenkins > Configure System.

Scroll to the Publish over SSH section.

Click Add and enter the following exact details for the lab server:

Name: stapp01

Hostname: stapp01

Username: tony

Password: Ir0nM@n (Check the box "Use password authentication, or use a different key" if it appears).

Remote Directory: /var/www/html

Click Test Configuration. You must see a Success message before proceeding.

Click Save.

🛠️ Step 3: Create the app-job and Add the Choice Parameter
From the Jenkins dashboard, click New Item.

Enter the name app-job, select Freestyle project, and click OK.

In the General section, check This project is parameterized.

Click Add Parameter and select Choice Parameter.

Configure it as follows:

Name: Branch

Choices: (enter each on a new line)

text
version1
version2
version3
📂 Step 4: Configure the Custom Workspace (The Critical Fix)
Scroll down to the Advanced Project Options section.

Check the box for Use custom workspace.

In the Directory field, enter exactly: /var/lib/jenkins/${Branch}

Why this works: For this specific lab environment, the ${Branch} syntax is confirmed to resolve correctly, creating directories like /var/lib/jenkins/version1. 
This avoids the common mistake of hardcoding a path or using an incorrect variable format.

📥 Step 5: Configure Source Code Management (Git)
In the Source Code Management section, select Git.

Enter the Repository URL for the web_app repository on Gitea (copy the HTTPS clone URL from the Gitea UI).

Add Credentials if needed (e.g., username sarah, password Sarah_pass123).

In the Branches to build section, change the Branch Specifier to exactly: */$Branch

Why this works: The Git plugin requires the $Branch (not ${Branch}) format to dynamically read the parameter value during the SCM checkout phase.

🚀 Step 6: Configure Post-Build Deployment (Publish Over SSH)
Scroll to the Post-build Actions section.

Click Add post-build action and select Send build artifacts over SSH.

In the SSH Server dropdown, select the stapp01 server you configured.

In the Transfers section, set:

Source files: ** (This ensures all files from the workspace are transferred, not just index.html).

Remove prefix: (leave blank).

Remote directory: ( leave blank if the global config already sets this).

Click Save.

✅ Step 7: Build and Verify
On the app-job page, click Build with Parameters.

Select version1 from the Branch dropdown and click Build.

Check the Console Output. You should see Jenkins:

Creating the workspace /var/lib/jenkins/version1.

Checking out the version1 branch from Git.

Successfully transferring files to /var/www/html on stapp01 via SSH.

To confirm, click the App button or log in to stapp01 and check that the correct version's files are present in /var/www/html.

💡 Summary of Key Corrections
Workspace Path: Use /var/lib/jenkins/${Branch} (not $Branch).

Git Branch Specifier: Use */$Branch (not ${Branch}).

SSH Authentication: Explicitly use password authentication with tony / Ir0nM@n to avoid the "Auth fail" error.

