Good catch on checking the filesystem — but there's a problem hidden in that listing that you need to fix before submitting. 🚨

🔍 What the Listing Reveals
text
-rw-r--r-- 1 tony tony   34 Nov 25 10:35 index.html
drwxr-xr-x 3 tony tony 4096 Nov 25 10:51 var
Two things stand out:

index.html is dated 10:35, but the build you just ran completed at ~10:51. So the index.html you're seeing is not from your latest build — it's from an earlier deployment (likely the task's pre-seeded content or a previous attempt).

A stray var directory was created at 10:51 — the exact time of your latest build.

That var directory is the smoking gun for a classic Publish Over SSH misconfiguration: a doubled remote path. Your latest build actually deployed the files to:

text
/var/www/html/var/www/html/index.html
instead of:

text
/var/www/html/index.html
🐛 Why This Happened
This occurs when both of these fields are set to the same path:

Global config (Manage Jenkins > Configure System > Publish over SSH > stapp01 > Remote Directory): /var/www/html

Job config (Post-build Actions > Send build artifacts over SSH > Transfers > Remote directory): /var/www/html

The plugin appends the job-level remote directory to the global one, producing /var/www/html + /var/www/html = /var/www/html/var/www/html/. That's where your var folder came from.

✅ How to Fix It
Step 1 — Confirm the misplacement (optional but reassuring):

bash
ls -R /var/www/html/var
You should see www/html/index.html nested inside it, confirming the doubled path.

Step 2 — Fix the job config in Jenkins:

Open app-job > Configure.

Scroll to Post-build Actions > Send build artifacts over SSH.

In the Transfers block, clear the "Remote directory" field entirely (leave it blank).

Click Save.

The global /var/www/html will now be applied only once, placing files directly in /var/www/html/.

Step 3 — Clean up the stray directory on stapp01:

bash
rm -rf /var/www/html/var
Step 4 — Rebuild and verify:

In Jenkins, click Build with Parameters > select version1 > Build.

On stapp01, run:

bash
ls -l /var/www/html
cat /var/www/html/index.html
You should now see only index.html with a fresh timestamp (matching the new build time), and no var directory.

🎯 Why This Matters for Grading
The task explicitly says to deploy "under /var/www/html directory." A nested /var/www/html/var/www/html/ path does not satisfy that requirement, even though the build reports SUCCESS. The grader will likely check the exact contents of /var/www/html, so the stray var folder and the stale index.html timestamp would both count against you.

💡 Quick Recap
Issue	Cause	Fix
var/ directory appeared in /var/www/html	Remote directory set in both global and job config	Leave job-level Remote Directory blank
index.html is stale (10:35, older than build)	Latest build wrote to the wrong nested path	Clear the stray var/ and rebuild
Once you clear the job's remote directory field, delete the var folder, and rebuild, the index.html in /var/www/html will be freshly written by your job, and the task will be genuinely complete. 👍

